 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law and the judiciary law, in relation\nto human trafficking courts",
    "basePrintNo": "S7836",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7836-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 43,
       "fullName": "Kathleen A. Marchione",
       "imgName": "435_kathleen_a._marchione.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 435,
       "sessionMemberId": 940,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "MARCHIONE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§170.15 & 180.20, CP L; amd §212, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7836\n\nSPONSOR: LANZA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the criminal procedure law and the judiciary law, in\nrelation to human trafficking courts\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow any county, outside of a city having a population of one\nmillion or more, the ability to move an action pending in a local crimi-\nnal court to a court of special concern based upon the status of the\ndefendants or the victim as a victim of domestic violence.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 5 to section 170.15 of the criminal\nprocedure law, to allow for the removal process of a misdemeanor action\npending in local criminal court to a court designated as a human traf-\nficking court.\n\nSection 2 adds a new subdivision 4 to section 180.20 of the criminal\nprocedure law, to allow for the removal process of a felony action pend-\ning in a local criminal court to a court designated as a human traffick-\ning court.\n\nSection 3 adds a new paragraph w to section 212 of the judiciary law to\nadd as a function of the chief administrator of the courts the authority\nto establish human trafficking courts deemed needed.\n\nSection 4 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2013, under the direction of former Chief Judge of the New York Court\nof Appeals, Jonathan Lippman, New York implemented the trailblazing\nHuman Trafficking Intervention Court (HTIC) Initiative, establishing 11\nspecial human trafficking criminal courts throughout the state - one for\neach of the five boroughs of New York City, and six in Long Island and\nupstate. These courts were created to provide alternatives to incarcera-\ntion for people arrested on prostitution charges, premised on the under-\nstanding that many of the defendants were also victims of human traf-\nficking.\n\nLike existing drug treatment courts, these HTICs link victims of human\ntrafficking and commercial sexual exploitation to crucial services that\nare appropriate for their individual situations, including counseling,\njob training, education, housing, medical assistance, immigration\nservices and substance abuse and mental health treatment.  Through an\nextensive collaboration of dedicated judges, prosecutors, defense\nlawyers, and service providers, the goal is to halt the systematic\npattern of shuffling defendants through courtrooms without addressing\nthe underlying cause of why they were arrested in the first place.\n\nHowever, unlike drug courts, which were created by statute in 2000, to\nact as hubs for the drug caseloads for their respective counties, four\nof the six current HTIC courts outside of NYC lack jurisdiction to see\n\ncases that originate outside of the local criminal courts where they are\nphysically situated.\n\nTo illustrate, the HTIC court in Westchester County, created as a part\nof the criminal division of the Yonkers City Court, may only see those\ncases where the arrests for prostitution occurred within the confines of\nYonkers, not those that occurred in a neighboring locality or elsewhere\nin the county. The same scenario applies for the Erie County HTIC court\nlocated in the Buffalo City Court, the Monroe County HTIC court in the\nRochester City Court, and the Onondaga County HTIC court in Syracuse\nCity Court.\n\nThe result is a system that leaves many victims of human trafficking who\nwould have benefited from these therapeutic court protocols in the cold.\nThis legislation would amend the criminal procedure law and the judici-\nary law to permit the removal of prostitution cases pending in the local\ncriminal courts to another local criminal court in the same county that\nhas been or may be established as a human trafficking court.\n\nIn New York, we have taken significant strides to address the problem of\nhuman trafficking. We must enact this additional measure to allow the\nspecial services these human trafficking courts can provide to be made\nreadily available to those who are arrested on prostitution charges in\ncommunities that technically fall outside of the court's regular juris-\ndiction.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
